<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Tray.wxs — the RST tray in RST.msi (feature #17, spec #20 §1; F17 WU1).

  RST.Tray.exe is built once, not per Revit year (build/stage.sh tray ->
  build/Tray/), and installs with the Main feature whatever Revit years are
  detected (the launch condition already needs one):

    %LocalAppData%\Programs\RST\Tray\       RST.Tray.exe and its files
                                            (ComponentGroup TrayFiles, generated
                                            by gen-tray.ps1 from build/Tray/)
    HKMU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
        RST = "<TrayDir>RST.Tray.exe" plus the startup switch
                                            starts the tray at sign-in; HKMU is
                                            HKCU in this per-user package. Windows
                                            Installer removes it on uninstall.
                                            Task Manager's Startup toggle
                                            (StartupApproved) is Windows' own and
                                            is respected as is.

  On uninstall, repair and upgrade a running tray is closed first. Windows
  Installer's Restart Manager usually gets there first (WM_QUERYENDSESSION:
  the tray lets WPF end it cleanly). util:CloseApplication covers the rest,
  e.g. MSIRESTARTMANAGERCONTROL=Disable: it sends WM_CLOSE to RST.Tray.exe's
  top-level windows (the tray keeps a hidden one and exits cleanly on it)
  and waits up to Timeout seconds. It never terminates the process and never
  asks for a reboot: CloseApplication matches RST.Tray.exe by name across
  every session, so a forced end would reach other users' trays (and WU4's
  updater, which therefore runs as RST.TrayUpdater.exe: F17 WU4). The
  per-machine package adds its own path-exact end for trays in other
  sessions (RstEndTrays, below). ci-smoke.ps1 checks both close paths.
  Neither package lets Restart Manager restart a closed tray (RST.msi:
  MSIDISABLERMRESTART=1; RST-Machine.msi: Restart Manager off; Product.wxs):
  that restart would come from msiexec, elevated when msiexec is. After every
  successful install, repair or upgrade RstStartTray (below, SC-044) starts
  the tray for the signed-in user instead, never elevated. util:RemoveFolderEx removes the tray's
  download cache %LocalAppData%\RST\Tray\cache\ on a real uninstall (not when
  an upgrade removes the old version). The tray's settings and log are user
  data under %AppData%\RST\ and stay, like the rest of RST's user data.

  The per-Revit-year components, the Revit detection (decision #10) and the
  MajorUpgrade settings in Product.wxs are unchanged by this file.

  RST-Machine.msi (Scope=machine, F17 WU5) uses the same file with these
  differences:
    - TrayDir is C:\Program Files\RST\Tray\ (under Product.wxs's RstFolder);
      HKMU is HKLM there, so the Run value starts the tray for every user at
      sign-in. The generated file components get new auto GUIDs because their
      paths differ.
    - TrayStartup has its own fixed GUID (WU1 review N6): the per-user GUID
      with a key path that resolves to HKLM would break the component rules.
    - No RemoveFolderEx, neither RemoveTrayCache nor WU4's RemoveTrayLocal
      (both inside Scope=user below): %LocalAppData%\RST\Tray\ is per user,
      and an administrator's uninstall must never delete their own (WU4
      review DN5), nor can it reach every user's.
    - Restart Manager does not restart a closed tray (Product.wxs,
      MSIRESTARTMANAGERCONTROL=Disable), because it would restart it from this
      elevated installer (WU1 review N8). RstStartTray (below) starts it
      instead, unelevated, through the installing user's Explorer (SC-044);
      as SYSTEM it starts none, and each user's tray starts at sign-in.
    - Every signed-in user's tray is ended before the files go (SC-028).
      CloseApplication's WM_CLOSE reaches only trays on the installer's own
      desktop, so an uninstall, repair or upgrade run as SYSTEM in session 0
      (SCCM, Intune) closed none: Windows Installer renamed the in-use files,
      the trays ran on, and msiexec exited 0. CloseApplication's
      TerminateProcess is no answer: it matches by file name, so it would end
      per-user trays (%LocalAppData%\Programs\RST\Tray\) too. RstEndTrays
      (helper\EndTrays.cs, deferred, as SYSTEM, before RemoveFiles) ends
      exactly the RST.Tray processes whose image is [TrayDir]RST.Tray.exe, in
      every session. On a full uninstall it then asks Restart Manager
      whether anything else (Revit with the engine loaded) still uses a file
      under [RstFolder]. If so it
      sets WiX's restart marker, and Wix4CheckRebootRequired, which this
      package also runs on uninstall, makes msiexec return 3010. The upgrade
      path is the same: RemoveExistingProducts runs the old package's own
      uninstall, RstEndTrays included. CloseApplication stays first: a tray
      on the installer's desktop still exits cleanly on WM_CLOSE.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <Fragment>
    <?if $(var.Scope) = "user" ?>
    <StandardDirectory Id="LocalAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="LocalProgramsFolder" Name="Programs">
        <Directory Id="ProgramsRstFolder" Name="RST">
          <Directory Id="TrayDir" Name="Tray" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- The cache folder for RemoveFolderEx, which takes a property (not a
         directory id). LocalAppDataFolder is known from the start, so this
         runs right after AppSearch, before RemoveFoldersEx (before
         CostInitialize). -->
    <SetProperty Id="RSTTRAYCACHEDIR" Value="[LocalAppDataFolder]RST\Tray\cache\" After="AppSearch" Sequence="execute" />
    <!-- BEGIN per-user only (F17 WU4; review N8): the rest of the tray's non-roaming folder, the updater
         copy (updater\), msiexec's install logs (logs\) and the self-update result. Removed with the cache on a
         real uninstall only, so an RST upgrade run BY the updater copy never removes the folder it runs from. In a
         per-machine package this must not exist: an administrator's uninstall would delete THEIR own folder
         (inside Scope=user, with RemoveTrayLocal below; WU4 review DN5). -->
    <SetProperty Id="RSTTRAYLOCALDIR" Value="[LocalAppDataFolder]RST\Tray\" After="AppSearch" Sequence="execute" />
    <!-- END per-user only -->
    <?else?>
    <DirectoryRef Id="RstFolder">
      <Directory Id="TrayDir" Name="Tray" />
    </DirectoryRef>
    <?endif?>

    <!-- Only when files can change: a first install or upgrade, a repair,
         an uninstall, or the old version's removal in an upgrade. A no-op
         reinstall of the installed product (IT re-pushing the same RST.msi)
         replaces nothing, so it leaves a running tray alone (Restart Manager
         then has nothing to close either, and would not restart it). -->
    <util:CloseApplication Id="CloseRstTray" Target="RST.Tray.exe"
                           Condition="NOT Installed OR REINSTALL OR REMOVE OR UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE"
                           CloseMessage="yes" RebootPrompt="no" Timeout="10"
                           Description="RST is closing its tray app." />

    <ComponentGroup Id="Tray">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="TrayFiles" />
      <?if $(var.Scope) = "user" ?>
      <Component Id="TrayStartup" Directory="TrayDir" Guid="25F3A510-0393-43BC-9462-5E60E225F27B">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" Name="RST"
                       Type="string" Value="&quot;[TrayDir]RST.Tray.exe&quot; --startup" KeyPath="yes" />
        <util:RemoveFolderEx Id="RemoveTrayCache" On="uninstall" Property="RSTTRAYCACHEDIR"
                             Condition="NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
        <!-- BEGIN per-user only (F17 WU4; review N8): see RSTTRAYLOCALDIR above. -->
        <util:RemoveFolderEx Id="RemoveTrayLocal" On="uninstall" Property="RSTTRAYLOCALDIR"
                             Condition="NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
        <!-- END per-user only -->
      </Component>
      <?else?>
      <Component Id="TrayStartup" Directory="TrayDir" Guid="A8082E2F-7DD4-4101-88F4-69C0BB6FBC43">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKMU" Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" Name="RST"
                       Type="string" Value="&quot;[TrayDir]RST.Tray.exe&quot; --startup" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <?endif?>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <?if $(var.Scope) = "machine" ?>
    <!-- SC-028: end every signed-in user's tray from this package's folder,
         in any session, then ask for a restart (3010) if anything else still
         uses the package's files. See the header and helper\EndTrays.cs.
         Deferred and not impersonated: it runs as SYSTEM, which can end a
         process in any session. Return="ignore": it never stops an uninstall.
         The same condition as CloseRstTray, so a no-op reinstall leaves the
         trays alone. "[RstFolder]." keeps the quoted argument from ending in
         a backslash. [REMOVE] is ALL only on a full uninstall, the one
         case it checks for files still in use (review N2). Each run writes
         its own log, [TempFolder]RST-Machine-EndTrays-<time>-<pid>.log (as
         SYSTEM: C:\Windows\Temp, or C:\Windows\SystemTemp). -->
    <Binary Id="RstEndTraysExe" SourceFile="$(var.RstEndTraysExe)" />
    <CustomAction Id="RstEndTrays" BinaryRef="RstEndTraysExe"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[TrayDir]RST.Tray.exe&quot; &quot;[RstFolder].&quot; &quot;[TempFolder]RST-Machine-EndTrays.log&quot; &quot;[REMOVE]&quot;"
                  Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="ignore" />
    <?endif?>

    <!-- SC-044 (decision #45): once an install, repair or upgrade has
         succeeded, start the tray for the signed-in user, hidden (startup
         switch), as that user and never elevated (helper\StartTray.cs). An
         immediate custom action after InstallFinalize: the install is already
         committed when it runs, and Return="ignore", so it can never fail or
         roll one back. It starts none as SYSTEM or in session 0 (a deployment
         tool: each user's tray starts at sign-in), none while a tray or RST's
         self-updater already holds the tray's mutex (a reinstall over a running
         tray, a self-update: the updater starts the tray itself), and none
         from an elevated process unless this user's Explorer can start it, so
         the tray gets Explorer's own unelevated token. Not on an uninstall,
         nor in the old version's removal during an upgrade (REMOVE is ALL in
         both). RST-Machine.msi run by RST-Setup.exe (RSTFROMSETUP) skips it:
         setup's RemoveUserRst helper starts the tray once it has removed the
         user's own RST, whose removal closes every RST.Tray.exe by name. Each
         run writes RST-StartTray-<time>-<pid>.log in the installer's TEMP. -->
    <Binary Id="RstStartTrayExe" SourceFile="$(var.RstStartTrayExe)" />
    <CustomAction Id="RstStartTray" BinaryRef="RstStartTrayExe"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[TrayDir]RST.Tray.exe&quot; &quot;[TempFolder]RST-StartTray.log&quot;"
                  Execute="immediate" Return="ignore" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <?if $(var.Scope) = "user" ?>
      <Custom Action="RstStartTray" After="InstallFinalize" Condition="NOT REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
      <?else?>
      <Custom Action="RstEndTrays" Before="RemoveFiles"
              Condition="NOT Installed OR REINSTALL OR REMOVE OR UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
      <!-- WiX schedules its CheckRebootRequired for install and repair only
           (NOT REMOVE~="ALL"); an uninstall that leaves files in use must say
           so too. -->
      <Custom Action="override Wix4CheckRebootRequired_X64" After="InstallFinalize" Condition="VersionNT &gt; 400" />
      <Custom Action="RstStartTray" After="InstallFinalize" Condition="NOT REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT RSTFROMSETUP" />
      <?endif?>
    </InstallExecuteSequence>
  </Fragment>

</Wix>
